<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="ebe6f0af">&lt;p&gt;
    According to the PMI, “project management is the application of knowledge, skills, tools, and techniques to project
    activities to meet the project requirements.”&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    PMI describes effective project management as applying the knowledge and skills from at least five areas of expertise:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Application area knowledge, standards, and regulations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Understanding the project environment (for example, the culture and environment of the organization)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        General management knowledge and skills
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Interpersonal skills
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Managing a project typically includes the following:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Identifying requirements
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Addressing various stakeholder needs, concerns and expectations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Balancing competing project constraints (e.g., scope, quality, schedule, budget, resources and risk)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Project management is characterized by a work plan that outlines tasks, resources, and time frames. Due to the
    potential for change within the project life cycle, practitioners should anticipate a project work plan that is
    iterative and goes through progressive elaboration during the process.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Project Management Methodology&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A project management methodology defines the practices, techniques, procedures and rules specific to the discipline of
    project management. The existence of a project management methodology is required in order to deliver projects
    consistently across an organization. The methodology is intended to be a practical guide for PMs. Included in the
    methodology is the definition of what tasks need to be performed on a project and by whom, as well as when and how to
    perform the tasks.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Our project management methodology is based on the PMBOK and also incorporates some of the concepts of PRINCE2
    (Projects IN Controlled Environments) specifically around governance and controls where PMI content is less
    developed.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Project Management Fundamentals&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The PMI’s PMBOK defines project management as being performed through the use of project management processes which aim
    to minimize costs and maximize quality and focus primarily on the management of scope, schedule, cost, quality and risk
    of the project. This, in turn, helps the PM to keep the project on track and in control, deliver the defined benefits,
    and meet project objectives.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
